{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011Cfnx3ebCdp3thpkWbKmVL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Perfect! Now I understand the structure:\n- `LineItem` has `sku`, `unit_price`, and `quantity`\n- The `Inventory` class needs a `reserve` method that takes a list of `LineItem`\n- It should decrement stock for each SKU\n- If any SKU is unknown or has insufficient stock, it should raise an error and leave all stock unchanged (all-or-nothing behavior)\n- I need to add a new error class for insufficient stock if needed\n\nLooking at the existing tests, I should probably add tests for the `reserve` method.\n\nFirst, I need to check if there's already an error class for insufficient stock. Looking at `app/errors.py`, there are:\n- `OrderError` (base)\n- `InvalidQuantity`\n- `UnknownSku`\n\nI'll need to add an `InsufficientStock` error class.\n\nNow let me implement the `reserve` method:\n\n```python\ndef reserve(self, items: list[LineItem]) -> None:\n    # First, validate all items and calculate total needed\n    needed = {}\n    for item in items:\n        if item.sku not in self._stock:\n            raise UnknownSku(item.sku)\n        if item.sku not in needed:\n            needed[item.sku] = 0\n        needed[item.sku] += item.quantity\n    \n    # Check if we have enough stock for all items\n    for sku, quantity_needed in needed.items():\n        if self._stock[sku] < quantity_needed:\n            raise InsufficientStock(sku, quantity_needed, self._stock[sku])\n    \n    # All checks passed, decrement stock\n    for sku, quantity_needed in needed.items():\n        self._stock[sku] -= quantity_needed\n```\n\nWait, I need to check the signature of the `InsufficientStock` error. Let me think about what parameters it should have. Looking at `UnknownSku(sku)`, it seems to take a single argument. For `InsufficientStock`, I could take `(sku, needed, available)` but I need to check how other erro